And then in the finale you understand what happened.
然后在结局里你才会明白发生了什么。

00:16:54.250 --> 00:17:07.849
And I, there's a series of scenes with me and Kobe that starts in the restaurant where he had stolen the blue French horn for earlier in the pilot.
而且，我和科比有一系列场景，是从那家餐厅开始的，他在试播集中曾为她偷过那只蓝色的法国号。

00:17:07.849 --> 00:17:16.650
And then there was an actual proposal that we talk about, the couple that you see get engaged in the show, that was him actually proposing his wife.
然后还有一个真正的求婚，我们谈到过，你在剧里看到的那对订婚的情侣，其实就是他本人在向他的妻子求婚。

00:17:16.650 --> 00:17:19.369
That's an actual real-life marriage proposal, yes.
那是一次真正的现实生活中的求婚，是的。

00:17:19.369 --> 00:17:25.049
They're still married, three daughters were thrilled to have learned this.
他们现在依然结婚在一起，育有三个女儿，得知这个消息我们非常兴奋。

00:17:25.049 --> 00:17:34.809
And then the scenes, the breakup scenes where they're telling Barney and popping back to Robin's apartment, I was really proud of me and Kobe.
然后就是那些分手戏，他们告诉巴尼并回到罗宾公寓的场景，我为我和科比的表现感到非常骄傲。

00:17:34.809 --> 00:17:39.049
Like, I thought they were really great, well-acted scenes.
我觉得那是真的很棒、很有演技张力的场景。

00:17:39.049 --> 00:17:41.289
I know it's a little weird to say, like, those scenes.
我知道这样评价自己的表演可能有点奇怪。

00:17:41.289 --> 00:17:42.130
It's not weird.
一点也不奇怪。

00:17:42.130 --> 00:17:42.950
They're so good.
真的太棒了。

00:17:42.950 --> 00:17:43.789
They're so good.
太棒了。

00:17:43.789 --> 00:17:44.630
We're so good together.
我们俩配合得太好了。

00:17:44.630 --> 00:17:49.009
And we had like two years of history kind of fooling it.
我们当时有两年的默契积累来支撑这些拍摄。

00:17:49.009 --> 00:17:51.130
And I really, I just loved it.
我真的很喜欢。

00:17:51.130 --> 00:18:00.809
And I loved, you know, I loved what a good friend Ted is to Marshall up until he can't take it anymore when Lily leaves.
而且我喜欢，你知道的，我喜欢泰德对马歇尔有多好，直到莉莉离开后他再也无法忍受。

00:18:00.809 --> 00:18:12.690
And I love the, I had forgotten how much Lily leaving for San Francisco and breaking Marshall's heart reverberates throughout the entire second season.
我也喜欢——我曾经忘记了莉莉离开去旧金山并心碎了马歇尔这件事，对整个第二季产生了多么深远的影响。

00:18:12.690 --> 00:18:16.529
Like my memory of it was like she comes back and all is forgiven and it's not that way at all.
因为我的记忆里好像是她回来了，一切都被原谅了，但事实根本不是这样的。

00:18:16.529 --> 00:18:17.369
It's not.
根本不是。

00:18:17.369 --> 00:18:18.930
And I thought that was really great.
我觉得这真的很棒。

00:18:18.930 --> 00:18:24.890
I mean, some of it I noticed like, oh, I'm really proud of us as actors, but I'm also a writer and a director.
我是说，其中有些部分我注意到，哦，我真的为我们作为演员感到骄傲，但我同时也是一名编剧和导演。

00:18:24.890 --> 00:18:30.930
So when I watch it now, I'm really intrigued and excited by like talking to Craig about.
所以现在当我回看时，我会和克雷格交流，并对这些感到非常着迷和兴奋。

00:18:30.930 --> 00:18:32.250
Like, where'd you come up with that?
比如，这个你是怎么想出来的？

00:18:32.250 --> 00:18:36.569
Like, that was so smart to like plant that seed in this episode and have it pay off three episodes later.
比如，在这个集数里埋下这个伏笔，然后在三集后得到呼应，这真是太聪明了。

00:18:36.569 --> 00:18:48.809
And just how they were playing the long game while also trying to satisfy the network wanting these to be episodes that you could just tune in on any random Monday night and like, but they were trying to tell this serialized story.
以及他们是如何在推进长线剧情的同时，还要努力满足电视台的要求——让这些剧集成为那种你随便在哪个周一晚上打开就能看的一集，但他们同时又在努力讲述一个连续的 serialized 故事。

00:18:48.809 --> 00:19:09.509
So it's a cool thing if you're interested in making something, especially a long-form television show that goes for nine seasons, it's really like a masterclass listening to Craig talk about what was going on in the writer's room, how they cracked the story, where they were headed, and when they didn't even know where they were headed and what they did in those situations.
所以这是一件很酷的事，如果你对创作感兴趣，尤其是创作一部长达九季的长篇电视剧，听克雷格讲述编剧室里发生的事、他们是如何构思故事走向、他们要去往何方、在甚至不知道方向时他们是如何应对的，这真的就像是一堂大师课。

00:19:09.509 --> 00:19:12.230
Yeah, Craig, let me bring this one to you.
好的，克雷格，我把这个问题抛给你。

00:19:12.230 --> 00:19:20.220
I mean, they're, I mean, this for everyone, obviously, but let's go, I love these, they're really big questions here.
我是说，这些，当然这是针对所有人的，不过让我们来看看，我喜欢这些问题，这里有很多非常宏大的问题。

00:19:21.700 --> 00:19:23.180
Let's have this one.
我们就选这个吧。

00:19:23.180 --> 00:19:28.180
What lesson from the series has impacted you the most?
剧中的哪个教训对你的影响最深？

00:19:28.220 --> 00:19:29.059
Oh, that's another tough one.
哦，这也是个难题。

00:19:29.059 --> 00:19:30.900
Let's do from the first two seasons.
我们就从前两季里面选吧。

00:19:30.900 --> 00:19:33.740
I know, right, we're in the first two seasons.
我懂的，对，我们还在讨论前两季。

00:19:33.740 --> 00:19:37.299
So, okay, I'll play off with Josh just said, and thank you, Josh, for those lovely words.
好吧，我就接着刚才乔希的话说，谢谢乔希刚才那些温暖的话。

00:19:37.299 --> 00:19:52.380
And like you guys, you and, it is such a writer's dream to write scenes like what you and Colby acted in the sequence you just described and watch it be elevated beyond anything you could have dreamed for that is just such a gift for everybody that wrote on that show.
像你们一样，你和，能够写出像你和科比在刚才描述的那个序列中所演绎的那些场景，并看着它被提升到你为之梦想的最高境界之外，对于所有为这部剧写作的编剧来说，这简直就是一种巨大的恩赐。

00:19:52.380 --> 00:19:55.819
So you guys believe me, we were in awe of you guys there.
所以你们相信我，我们在你们面前时满怀敬畏。

00:19:56.900 --> 00:20:05.420
I would say, Josh just sort of said it, we had this awkward experience at the end of season one where the network said, make it less serialized.
我想说，正如乔希刚才所说的那样，我们在第一季结束时有过一次尴尬的经历，当时电视台说：让剧情少一点连贯性吧。

00:20:05.420 --> 00:20:10.980
You've done season one, and you did it like a weird little soap opera, right?
你们拍完了第一季，把它拍得像一部奇怪的小肥皂剧，对吧？

00:20:10.980 --> 00:20:14.940
You had your, you're expecting your audience to remember week to week what happened.
你们在要求观众记住每周发生了什么。

00:20:14.940 --> 00:20:26.859
It's exactly what Josh just said, like in season two, you see these echoes and reverberations of the consequences of this really intense emotional thing that happened between Marshall Millie and the way it influences the whole group.
这正是乔希刚才所说的，就像在第二季中，你会看到马歇尔和莉莉之间发生的这件非常强烈的情感事件所带来的后果的余波和回响，以及它如何影响了整个群体。

00:20:26.859 --> 00:20:32.500
Now we were borderline yelled at to stop doing the end of season one.
当时我们几乎被大喊着要求停止第一季结尾的那种做法。

00:20:32.500 --> 00:20:51.640
We was strongly suggested perhaps we might not want to do that and we might want to be more like the others that comes on CBS that were these little standalone things and every week everyone had amnesia and the week before it never happened and I'm really, the answer to that question is, I'm really proud the lesson that we learned.
有人强烈建议或许我们不该那样做，我们也许应该更像CBS播出的其他剧集那样，做成那种小型的独立剧集，每周每个人都患有健忘症，前一周发生的事仿佛从未发生过。真的，这个问题的答案是，对于我们学到的这个教训，我感到非常自豪。

00:20:51.640 --> 00:20:53.640
Fuck that.
去他的。

00:20:53.640 --> 00:20:57.039
Now, let's write the show we want to write.
现在，我们要写我们想写的剧。

00:20:57.039 --> 00:20:59.480
Let's write the show we want to see.
我们要写我们自己想看的剧。

00:20:59.480 --> 00:21:12.960
It's right how we would want to watch a TV show, which at that time was binging TV shows on DVDs because it was pre-streaming because we're a thousand years old, but it turned out to be a really safe bet, a really smart bet because people now consume TV very differently.
我们要按照我们自己希望的方式来观看一部电视剧，当时那是靠在DVD上刷剧，因为那还是流媒体出现之前的年代，虽然我们已经老得像有一千岁了，但事实证明这是一个非常稳妥的赌注，一个非常聪明的赌注，因为现在人们消费电视剧的方式已经完全不同了。

00:21:12.960 --> 00:21:22.119
And that happened during our run and people caught up to it on Netflix when it got on Netflix or sort of like season four or so, and our bet paid off.
这在我们的播出期间发生了，当它大约在第四季左右登陆Netflix时，人们在Netflix上赶上了进度，我们的豪赌得到了回报。

00:21:22.119 --> 00:21:25.480
Our bet was we have a smart audience.
我们的赌注是：我们有一群聪明的观众。

00:21:25.480 --> 00:21:27.759
They want to watch things that matter week to week.
他们想要看每周都有实质内容的东西。

00:21:27.759 --> 00:21:39.279
They want to watch the show have its own internal memory and they want to see consequences and reverberations and echoes and setups and callbacks and twists and turns and call forwards where you see something and you go, what?
他们希望看到这部剧拥有自己的内在记忆，他们希望看到因果关系、余波、回响、铺垫、呼应、反转以及前瞻性的伏笔——当你看到某个画面时会说：什么？

00:21:39.279 --> 00:21:40.279
What is that?
那是什么？

00:21:40.279 --> 00:21:42.960
Like a flash forward and then it's explained to episodes later.
就像一个闪回或者闪现，然后在好几集之后得到解释。

00:21:42.960 --> 00:21:53.720
We did the thing of right for your smartest audience, right for your smartest, best, most sensitive audience and thank you, how I met your mother fans for approving us right because what a gift.
我们所做的事情是：为最聪明的观众写作，为最聪明、最好、最敏感的观众写作。《老爸老妈浪漫史》的粉丝们，谢谢你们认可我们，因为这真是太棒的礼物了。

00:21:53.720 --> 00:21:59.599
Well, also I think you guys were writing about, like I call the, how I met your mother, you are like 27 to 36, right?
嗯，而且我认为你们当时写的是，就像我称呼《老爸老妈浪漫史》的那样，你们正处于27岁到36岁左右，对吧？

00:21:59.599 --> 00:22:04.559
Like the, that's how old the characters were when in the show.
就像，那是剧中角色的年龄。

00:22:04.559 --> 00:22:11.640
But if you look at those years, your 20s and your early 30s, those years are very consequential.
但如果你回顾那些年，你的20多岁和30岁出头，那些年是非常具有决定性的。

00:22:11.640 --> 00:22:16.319
And everything you've done stacks up towards the next thing.
你所做的一切都在为接下来的事情做积累。

00:22:16.319 --> 00:22:19.000
So it's like, you can't, I think you use the term etch a sketch.
所以这就像是，你不能——我想你用过“ etch a sketch”（神奇画板）这个词。

00:22:19.000 --> 00:22:22.200
Like you can't like shake the etch a sketch and start a little bit.
就像你不能把画板摇一摇然后从头开始一样。

00:22:22.200 --> 00:22:31.680
Like it has to be, things have consequences in meaning and you make your next move based on either the success or failure of your last move.
它必须是连续的，事情会产生后果和意义，你的下一步行动是基于你上一步行动的成功或失败来做出的。

00:22:31.680 --> 00:22:41.579
So I think it's a more emotionally honest show that you had a memory, like the flame of memory going the whole time, at least in which the whole show is a memory.
所以我觉得这是一部在情感上更诚实的剧，它拥有一种记忆，就像记忆的火焰自始至终都在燃烧，整部剧本身就是一段记忆。

00:22:41.579 --> 00:22:45.819
It's a show about how at that time of real life, life starts to acquire meaning.
这是一部关于在真实生活的那个时期，生活开始被赋予意义的剧集。

00:22:45.819 --> 00:22:46.660
Yes.
是的。

00:22:46.660 --> 00:22:48.500
And consequence.
还有结果。

00:22:48.500 --> 00:22:49.980
The etch a sketch part is over.
那种可以随时摇晃清空的神奇画板时代已经结束了。

00:22:49.980 --> 00:22:50.819
Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.
对，对，对，对。

00:22:50.819 --> 00:22:51.660
That's right.
没错。

00:22:51.660 --> 00:22:55.019
It's more grooved, you know.
更有节奏感了，你知道的。

00:22:55.019 --> 00:23:06.059
Two things that I, just lines that stick out for me, I was sharing something about how in the middle of the night, like you shouldn't trust your thoughts when you wake up in the middle of the night.
有两件事，也就是那些让我印象深刻的话，我之前分享过关于半夜醒来的事，比如半夜醒来时，你真不该相信自己的想法。

00:23:06.059 --> 00:23:08.859
And it actually occurred to me like nothing good happens after 2 a.m.
我突然意识到，其实“凌晨两点之后没有好事发生”这句话。

00:23:08.859 --> 00:23:16.619
Is a good phrase not just for, you know, go home, but also like if you wake up after 2 a.m.
不仅仅适用于提醒你“该回家了”，还适用于如果你在凌晨两点后醒来。

00:23:16.619 --> 00:23:19.339
Nothing good is really happening in your head.
你脑子里其实也没什么好事。

00:23:19.339 --> 00:23:25.460
Like most of the time that's where your anxiety strikes or your worst-case scenario, thinking happens.
因为大多数时候，焦虑或最坏情况的胡思乱想就是在那时候袭来的。

00:23:25.460 --> 00:23:28.111
So I reframe nothing good happens after 2 a.m.
所以我把“凌晨两点后没有好事发生”

00:23:28.111 --> 00:23:31.339
for myself, which is like, nothing good happens up here.
这句话重新解读为：我的脑子里不会有什么好事发生。

00:23:31.339 --> 00:23:35.339
That's brilliant, I'm stealing that because that's when bad shit happens in my mind.
太绝了，我要借用这句话，因为每当我的大脑里出现破事时，就是这个时候。

00:23:35.339 --> 00:23:36.180
Oh my God, yeah.
天哪，是的。

00:23:36.180 --> 00:23:37.579
But what do you then do with that?
那你接下来会怎么处理呢？

00:23:37.579 --> 00:23:38.420
How do you put it at least?
至少你是怎么把这种情绪排解掉的？

00:23:38.420 --> 00:23:39.259
Well, I try it right.
嗯，我会试着这样做。

00:23:39.259 --> 00:23:41.619
It is like by naming it, you shrink it by naming it.
这就好比给它起个名字，通过命名来削弱它的力量。

00:23:41.619 --> 00:23:46.099
I try to bracket everything as saying like, this is an unreliable narrator in my head.
我试着把所有这些想法归类为：我脑子里有个不可靠的叙述者。

00:23:46.099 --> 00:23:47.539
Ha ha ha ha.
哈哈哈哈哈。

00:23:47.539 --> 00:23:49.779
Like this is not someone to be trusted.
就好像这不是一个可以信任的人。

00:23:49.779 --> 00:23:50.619
Yes.
没错。

00:23:50.619 --> 00:23:53.579
that that whatever that is, drawing is our erroneous.
不管那是什么，那种想法都是荒谬的。

00:23:53.579 --> 00:23:55.380
Like they're so bad.
太离谱了。

00:23:55.380 --> 00:23:56.380
Yes.
是的。

00:23:56.380 --> 00:23:58.740
So I try not to listen.
所以我尽量不去听。

00:23:58.740 --> 00:24:07.019
And I've spoken a lot about this, but in the pilot, Ted has the whole thing about, you know, it's not like I imagined my wedding.
我对此聊过很多次，在试播集中，泰德有一段台词，大意是，这可不像我幻想中的婚礼。

00:24:07.019 --> 00:24:10.859
And then he has this, you know, down to perfect detail.
然后他接着说，甚至连最完美的细节都想好了。

00:24:10.859 --> 00:24:11.980
He's imagined the wedding.
他连婚礼的场景都幻想好了。

00:24:11.980 --> 00:24:14.220
And then at the end he says, bam, no DJ, people will dance.
最后他说，砰，没有DJ，大家也会跳舞。

00:24:14.220 --> 00:24:15.140
I'm not gonna worry about it.
我才不操心这事呢。

00:24:15.140 --> 00:24:18.380
And I spoke about this in the podcast that the phrase people will dance.
我在播客里说过，“人们会跳舞”这句话。

00:24:18.380 --> 00:24:26.859
I actually named my corporation, people will dance because to me, it's almost like the opposite of nothing good out.
我甚至把我的公司命名为“人们会跳舞”，因为对我来说，它几乎就是“绝无好事”的反义词。

00:24:26.859 --> 00:24:29.859
It's a rallying cry for the optimist, right?
它是乐观主义者的战斗呐喊，对吧？

00:24:29.859 --> 00:24:30.859
Yeah.
对。

00:24:30.859 --> 00:24:36.819
Like, life will take care of itself, there will be joy, there will be celebration, people will dance and I don't have to worry about it.
就好比，生活会顺其自然，会有欢乐，会有庆祝，人们会跳舞，而我根本不用为此担忧。

00:24:36.819 --> 00:24:41.380
And people have gotten tattoos around that phrase because of your celebration.
因为你们的这种庆祝，有人甚至把这句话文在了身上。

00:24:41.380 --> 00:24:45.339
You called that phrase out, you're the one that underlined that phrase.
你喊出了这句话，你是那个给这句话划重点的人。

00:24:45.339 --> 00:24:46.339
After playing music or sorry.
在播放音乐之后，或者抱歉。

00:24:46.339 --> 00:24:49.539
And they say, can you write people will dance, I'm going to get a tattoo of it.
他们说，你能写下“人们会跳舞”吗？我要去纹个身。

00:24:49.539 --> 00:24:57.299
Which is the epitome of the statement right there dancing we've we've just been asked by J.S.
这简直就是这句话的精髓所在，话说回来，J.S.

00:24:57.299 --> 00:25:04.289
Crogs when the people will dance merch will be dropping and you know we'll uh you guys come to the website and buy it then maybe we'll do it.
克罗格斯刚刚问我们“人们会跳舞”的周边商品什么时候上架，你知道的，我们会……大家去网站买的话，我们可能就会推出了。

00:25:04.289 --> 00:25:05.975
I mean that would be a cool dance.
我是说，那会是一件很酷的舞衣。

00:25:05.975 --> 00:25:07.661
They would be a really cool shirt.
那会是一件非常酷的衬衫。

00:25:07.661 --> 00:25:12.122
If I saw someone and I didn't know what that phrase meant I'd be like that's a cool shirt.
如果我看到一个人穿，而我不知道这句话的意思，我会觉得：这衬衫真酷。

00:25:12.122 --> 00:25:13.709
What's that what does that mean?
这是什么？这是什么意思？

00:25:13.709 --> 00:25:15.939
All right you intuitively know what it means.
好吧，你凭直觉就知道它是什么意思。

00:25:15.940 --> 00:25:17.293
Yeah you really know what it means.
对，你真的知道它是什么意思。

00:25:17.293 --> 00:25:18.259
All right we're doing it.
好吧，我们这就做。

00:25:18.259 --> 00:25:26.619
Okay, we've got Mexico, we've got Portugal, we've got Siberia, and the matching thing could be people with pants, pants.
好吧，我们有墨西哥的，我们有葡萄牙的，我们有西伯利亚的，与之搭配的可能是“穿裤子的人，裤子”。

00:25:26.619 --> 00:25:28.019
Is that anything?
这算什么梗吗？

00:25:28.019 --> 00:25:29.420
We'll come back to that later.
我们待会儿再聊这个。

00:25:29.420 --> 00:25:32.380
That's more of a, for the latter many years, it's one of us.
这更像是一个……为了接下来的很多年，这是属于我们其中一个人的梗。

00:25:32.380 --> 00:25:34.259
Yeah, you know what Dave did that.
对，你知道戴夫就是这么干的。

00:25:34.259 --> 00:25:35.259
It's a deep call.
这是一个很深的梗。

00:25:35.259 --> 00:25:40.900
By the way, Fatima.seif.mirz asks the important question.
顺便说一句，Fatima.seif.mirz 提出了一个重要问题。

00:25:40.900 --> 00:25:42.819
Alec, did you ever make a cameo?
亚历克，你客串过吗？

00:25:42.819 --> 00:25:45.900
Did you, is that you, is that you?
是你吗，那是你吗，那是你吗？

00:25:45.900 --> 00:25:48.759
Is anyone would ask for many of that question?
有人会问很多类似的问题吗？

00:25:48.759 --> 00:25:50.279
That I see right through that show.
我一眼就能看穿那个节目。

00:25:50.279 --> 00:25:51.240
You see right through that.
你一眼就能看穿。

00:25:51.240 --> 00:25:56.319
Okay, I am going to ask the question though, Jessica Wallace, XOXO, I see your question, and thank you.
好吧，不过我要问这个问题了，杰西卡·华莱士，XOXO，我看到你的问题了，谢谢你。

00:25:56.319 --> 00:25:58.319
You can answer the question.
你可以回答这个问题。

00:25:58.319 --> 00:26:01.279
No, oh, thanks, you know, I am on the show.
不，哦，谢谢，你知道的，我确实参演了那个节目。

00:26:01.279 --> 00:26:06.960
I play Todd in episode something or other in the first season, best prom ever.
我在第一季的某集里扮演托德，也就是“史上最棒的舞会”那一集。

00:26:06.960 --> 00:26:09.559
And you can go check that one out.
你们可以去看看那一集。

00:26:09.559 --> 00:26:10.720
Here's this is a nice question.
这是一个很棒的问题。

00:26:10.720 --> 00:26:16.200
We, I think I'm young people watch the show, right?
我们，我觉得年轻人也在看这个节目，对吧？

00:26:16.200 --> 00:26:24.279
And sometimes we're taking a little aback at the ages that are pitched as being appropriate to start with.
有时我们对开始观看该剧的合适年龄设定会感到有点惊讶。

00:26:24.279 --> 00:26:32.039
But Kush777 says, are there any lessons or messages you want young people or teenagers to take from the show?
但 Kush777 问：有什么教训或信息是你想让年轻人或青少年从这部剧里汲取的吗？

00:26:32.039 --> 00:26:37.119
I'm 16, and it is my favorite show.
我16岁，这是我最喜欢的节目。

00:26:37.119 --> 00:26:38.839
Josh, I mean, I have an answer.
乔什，我是说，我心里有答案。

00:26:38.839 --> 00:26:39.680
Do you have an answer?
你有什么答案吗？

00:26:39.680 --> 00:26:40.519
I think I know what the answer is.
我想我知道答案是什么。

00:26:40.519 --> 00:26:41.640
I could come up with an answer.
我能想出一个答案。

00:26:41.640 --> 00:26:42.799
But what do you think?
但你怎么看？

00:26:42.799 --> 00:26:43.799
What's yours?
你的看法是什么？

00:26:43.799 --> 00:26:45.359
Here's my answer.
我的答案是这样的。

00:26:45.359 --> 00:26:50.400
The world is so intense and dark right now.
现在的世界如此激烈和黑暗。

00:26:50.400 --> 00:27:01.839
I have such sympathy, empathy, heartache for young people right now, looking at the lack of sympathy and empathy in the world right now.
对于当下的年轻人，看到如今世界上缺乏同情心和共情能力，我感到非常同情、共情和心碎。

00:27:01.839 --> 00:27:24.949
And I just hope that when they look at a show that is about a future version of yourself looking back on all the times that feel chaotic and confusing and impossible, that there's some sense or hidden message there that says you're going to be okay, there's going to be a future version of you that gets through all this chaos and is there at the other side of it to tell your story.
我只是希望，当他们看到一部讲述未来的自己回首那些感到混乱、困惑和不可能的时光的剧集时，里面能有一种感觉或隐藏的信息，告诉你：你会没事的，未来的你一定会度过所有这些混乱，并走到事情的另一面去讲述你的故事。

00:27:24.949 --> 00:27:34.362
And I hope I like to believe that's why the show does speak to people who are in their teens and twenties and people are finding it in a younger generation.
我希望，我也愿意相信，这就是为什么这部剧能够真正触动十几岁、二十几岁的年轻人，以及年轻一代正在从中找到共鸣。

00:27:34.362 --> 00:27:36.413
I hope that it gives them comfort.
我希望这能给他们带来慰藉。

00:27:36.413 --> 00:27:48.240
It's a really hard moment to look ahead to the future, but I have to believe in my heart of parts that you're going to be telling the story of all this and you're going to come out the other side.
要展望未来是一个非常艰难的时刻，但我内心深处必须相信，你们将会讲述这一切的故事，并且你们会安然度过难关。

00:27:48.240 --> 00:27:49.240
I love that, Craig.
我太喜欢这个回答了，克雷格。

00:27:49.240 --> 00:27:51.240
Can I add something?
我能补充一点吗？

00:27:51.240 --> 00:27:52.240
Please.
请说。

00:27:52.240 --> 00:28:00.160
Um, I think Einstein said the thing about the only question you have to decide for yourself is if it's a friendly universe or not.
嗯，我想爱因斯坦说过这样一句话：你唯一需要自己决定的是，这是一个友好的宇宙，还是一个不友好的宇宙。

00:28:00.160 --> 00:28:01.160
Yeah.
对。

00:28:01.160 --> 00:28:02.160
Yeah.
对。

00:28:02.160 --> 00:28:07.119
And how I met your mother, I think one of the reasons it's so beloved, beloved, beloved.
而《老爸老妈浪漫史》，我认为它如此备受喜爱、备受喜爱、备受喜爱的原因之一。

00:28:07.119 --> 00:28:09.880
My wife and I have a disagreement about how to say that.
我和我妻子对这个词怎么读有分歧。

00:28:09.880 --> 00:28:10.880
What a weird argument.
多么奇怪的争论啊。

00:28:10.880 --> 00:28:11.880
Yeah.
对。

00:28:11.880 --> 00:28:12.720
biggest fight.
最大的争吵。

00:28:12.720 --> 00:28:14.400
I mean, knock down, drag out.
我是说，大打出手那种。

00:28:14.400 --> 00:28:22.339
You know, I think that how I met your mother posits that it's a friendly universe.
你知道的，我认为《老爸老妈浪漫史》认为这是一个友好的宇宙。

00:28:22.339 --> 00:28:23.779
Yes, it does.
是的，它的确如此。

00:28:23.779 --> 00:28:27.410
And I think that's so comforting to people.
我觉得这太让人感到宽慰了。

00:28:27.410 --> 00:28:31.769
I think it's also true, because I believe the universe is ultimately friendly.
我认为这也是事实，因为我相信宇宙归根结底是友好的。

00:28:31.769 --> 00:28:35.690
That doesn't mean that darkness doesn't have its day and doesn't have its moment.
但这并不意味着黑暗没有它猖獗或占据主导的时候。

00:28:35.690 --> 00:28:36.930
And that's in this show too?
而这在剧中也有体现，对吧？

00:28:36.930 --> 00:28:37.769
Yeah, yeah.
是啊，是啊。

00:28:37.769 --> 00:28:38.609
That is also in this show.
这节目里也有这个。

00:28:38.609 --> 00:28:39.450
Yeah.
对。

00:28:39.450 --> 00:28:44.319
But my friend sent me today, I'm gonna get real deep here for just a moment.
不过我朋友今天发给我一段话，我接下来要稍微深入探讨一下。

00:28:44.319 --> 00:28:46.039
Forgive me if this isn't your thing.
如果这不是你们喜欢的风格，请原谅。

00:28:46.039 --> 00:28:49.960
He sent me a quote from the Damapada, which is a Buddhist text.
他发给我一句来自《法句经》的话，那是一部佛教经典。

00:28:49.960 --> 00:28:50.799
And I love this.
我很喜欢这个。

00:28:50.799 --> 00:28:54.519
It says, in this world, hate never yet dispelled hate.
上面说，在这世上，恨永远无法消除恨。

00:28:54.519 --> 00:28:56.660
Only love dispells hate.
只有爱能消除恨。

00:28:56.660 --> 00:29:00.759
This is the law, ancient and inexhaustible.
这是永恒且不灭的法则。

00:29:00.759 --> 00:29:03.119
You two shall pass away knowing this.
你们俩也终将明白这一点而离去。

00:29:03.119 --> 00:29:04.640
How can you quarrel?
你们怎么还能争吵呢？

00:29:04.640 --> 00:29:11.000
However many holy words you read, however many you speak, what good will they do you if you do not act upon them?
无论你读了多少神圣的言语，无论你说了多少，如果你不付诸行动，它们对你有什么用呢？

00:29:11.000 --> 00:29:17.549
read as few words as you like and speak fewer, but act upon the law.
读你想读的少许文字，说更少的话，但要依循法则去行动。

00:29:17.549 --> 00:29:19.710
This law that love dispels hate.
也就是爱能消除恨这一法则。

00:29:19.710 --> 00:29:31.099
And I just, you know, it's such a dark moment and it's such a hard time to not fall into cynicism or feel that like darkness is winning, but it doesn't, it never will.
我只是觉得，你知道的，在这个如此黑暗的时刻，在这个很难不陷入愤世嫉俗、很难不觉得黑暗正在获胜的艰难时期，但它并没有，它永远不会。

00:29:31.099 --> 00:29:32.779
It ultimately, it just never will.
归根结底，它就是永远不会。

00:29:32.779 --> 00:29:36.250
And the thing I like about what that says, it's a law.
而且我喜欢它的地方在于，它是一个法则。

00:29:36.250 --> 00:29:37.089
I love that word.
我很喜欢“法则”这个词。

00:29:37.089 --> 00:29:38.250
It's a law there.
那是一个法则。

00:29:38.250 --> 00:29:40.569
Yeah, that's not like negotiable.
对，那是无可商量的。

00:29:40.569 --> 00:29:45.759
It's a law, and I feel like how I met your mother honors that law.
这是一个法则，我觉得《老爸老妈浪漫史》致敬了那个法则。

00:29:45.759 --> 00:29:46.960
Yeah, I think so.
对，我也这么觉得。

00:29:46.960 --> 00:29:47.799
I think so.
我也这么觉得。

00:29:47.799 --> 00:29:49.519
I think that is the law of how much a mother.
我觉得那就是《老爸老妈浪漫史》的法则。

00:29:49.519 --> 00:29:50.869
I agree.
我同意。

00:29:52.390 --> 00:29:57.390
Mark, underscore our rampage, our rampage.
马克，下划线，我们的狂飙，我们的狂飙。

00:29:57.589 --> 00:30:03.950
What's to know about, what lessons you learned about, I'm sorry, what lessons you learned about writing throughout the show.
关于……你想了解什么，关于……抱歉，关于你在整个节目中对编剧工作有什么心得体会。

00:30:03.950 --> 00:30:30.684
You sort of talked about the sticking to your gun's idea, but how about the actual act of story, structuring out a story you hadn't I mean you could talk a little bit about what TV you had written prior to this but nine years I hope you learned something what did you learn in the writing world and Josh too I mean in the midst of all this you wrote a couple of movies and then have kept have continued to write what lessons do you learn about writing throughout the show.
你之前谈到过坚持己见的想法，但关于故事的实际创作、构思一个你以前……我的意思是，你可以稍微聊聊在此之前你写过什么电视剧，但九年了，我希望你学到了一些东西，你在编剧界学到了什么？乔什也是，我的意思是，在这一切当中，你写了几部电影，并且一直在继续写作，在整个节目的创作过程中，你对写作有什么心得体会？

00:30:30.684 --> 00:30:36.575
The times we felt we were in trouble were the times where we we didn't quite have the story figured out.
我们觉得陷入困境的时候，往往是我们还没有把故事彻底理清的时候。

00:30:36.575 --> 00:30:38.331
It was always about that stage.
问题总是出在那个阶段。

00:30:38.331 --> 00:30:40.030
It was always about the story.
总是跟故事有关。

00:30:40.030 --> 00:30:57.532
It was always about what's on the big dryer raceboards, Act 1, Act 2, Act 3, and then how do we translate that to a really robust outline, like 10 pages, single spaced scene by scene outline, what here's every scene, here's why those scenes are there, here's how that scene starts, here's how that scene ends.
总是跟大白板上写的东西有关，第一幕、第二幕、第三幕，然后我们如何把它转化为一个非常详尽的大纲，比如10页单倍行距的逐场大纲，这里有每一个场景，为什么这些场景会在这里，这个场景是怎么开始的，这个场景是怎么结束的。

00:30:57.532 --> 00:31:03.026
And in the middle of those scenes later, Future U, this is Future U's problem, We'll write jokes.
至于这些场景中间的部分，未来的你——这是未来的你的问题——我们会去写笑话。

00:31:03.026 --> 00:31:10.729
And it's really fucking easy to write jokes when you know what the story is, and it's really fucking hard to write jokes when you don't.
当你清楚故事是什么的时候，写笑话他妈的真容易；而当你不知道时，写笑话他妈的真难。

00:31:10.729 --> 00:31:16.337
And when it's just freestanding jokes based on nothing, there are geniuses who can write like that.
如果只是毫无根据的独立笑话，确实有些天才能够那样写作。

00:31:16.337 --> 00:31:17.810
Jack Handy, deep thoughts.
杰克·汉迪，《深思》系列。

00:31:17.809 --> 00:31:20.337
Stephen Wright, the comedian, right?
喜剧演员斯蒂芬·赖特，对吧？

00:31:20.337 --> 00:31:26.164
There's like, there's geniuses that can out of nowhere have a perfect 12-word joke.
有些天才能够凭空想出一个完美的12个字的笑话。

00:31:26.164 --> 00:31:36.134
But boy, it is way easier to write jokes when you're writing about the story and running with the character and the situation, the jokes come.
但是天哪，当你围绕着故事创作、顺着角色和情境去写时，笑话自然就来了，写笑话要容易得多。

00:31:36.134 --> 00:31:37.468
It's not that hard.
没那么难。

00:31:37.468 --> 00:31:39.434
So that's my biggest lesson.
所以这是我最大的心得。

00:31:39.434 --> 00:31:40.627
It's about story.
关键在于故事。

00:31:40.627 --> 00:31:47.016
Create that shape, do the work ahead of time, worry about the dialogue and the jokes later.
先把那个骨架搭好，提前做好功课，对话和笑话的事以后再说。

00:31:47.016 --> 00:32:01.619
I learned a lot from you guys about the pleasure of withholding in some ways, like creating confusion and then the pleasure of of solving it, I also kind of learned, it's kind of a von, a Kurt Vonnegut thing.
我从你们身上学到了很多关于适时克制的乐趣，比如制造困惑，然后享受解开谜团的乐趣，我还学到了一点，这有点像库尔特·冯内古特的风格。

00:32:01.619 --> 00:32:08.200
He says like, create characters that your audience will love and then put them through hell.
他说过，创造一群你的观众会喜爱的角色，然后把他们扔进人间炼狱。

00:32:08.200 --> 00:32:11.480
Yes, yes, that's it, that's it.
没错，没错，就是这样，就是这样。

00:32:11.480 --> 00:32:17.180
And you have to love those characters and your heart has to be broken putting them through hell too or else you're just a sociopath.
你必须爱这些角色，当看到他们经历人间炼狱时，你的心也必须随之碎裂，否则你就是一个反社会者。

00:32:17.180 --> 00:32:21.839
Like you have to as a writer be like, I love them so much and I'm putting them through hell to learn something about them.
作为编剧，你必须觉得，我太爱他们了，我正让他们经历炼狱来了解他们的一些事情。

00:32:21.839 --> 00:32:26.240
And also to ultimately deposit them, hopefully in a better place than when they start.
并且最终，希望把他们带到一个比他们开始时更好的地方。

00:32:26.240 --> 00:32:31.119
somewhere better that they could never have gotten to without the hell part, without the Shawshank sewer pipe.
带到一个他们不经历炼狱、不经历《肖申克的救赎》里那根下水道管就绝无可能到达的更好地方。

00:32:32.559 --> 00:32:40.440
Carter is really, Carter Bay is a really, he's a master of risk confusing the audience.
卡特真的是，卡特·贝斯真的是，他是让观众感到困惑的大师。

00:32:40.440 --> 00:32:42.079
Carter's really good at that.
卡特在这方面真的很擅长。

00:32:42.079 --> 00:32:47.039
He's like, that sounded like a backwards compliment or something, I mean it as a high praise.
他会觉得，这话听起来像是在讽刺或者别的什么，我是由衷地高度赞扬。

00:32:47.039 --> 00:32:47.880
Oh yeah.
哦，对。

00:32:47.880 --> 00:32:50.440
They don't need to know what's happening for a minute.
有一会儿他们不需要知道发生了什么。

00:32:50.440 --> 00:32:54.000
You can plant things that are confusing and pay them off later.
你可以埋下一些让人困惑的伏笔，然后在后面揭晓。

00:32:54.000 --> 00:32:56.799
Carter was a real, it was and is a real master of that.
卡特真的是，过去是、现在也是这方面真正的行家。

00:32:56.799 --> 00:32:59.000
So I feel like I learned a lot from him.
所以我感觉我从他身上学到了很多。

00:32:59.000 --> 00:33:06.759
I had a singing teacher at NYU, Debbie Lapidus, who I just adored and continued to adore, but she, oh, you know, Alec, we did a thing with her.
我在纽约大学时有一个声乐老师，黛比·拉皮杜斯，我非常崇拜她，至今依然崇拜她，但她，哦，你知道的，亚历克，我们跟她合作过一个项目。

00:33:06.759 --> 00:33:07.599
I did.
我做过。

00:33:07.599 --> 00:33:08.440
I did.
我做过。

00:33:08.440 --> 00:33:11.519
But she always used to say, play to the smartest people in the room.
但她总是说，要迎合房间里最聪明的人。

00:33:11.519 --> 00:33:12.359
Yeah.
对。

00:33:12.359 --> 00:33:14.440
And it was like the best thing I heard in grad school.
那是我在研究生院听到的最好的一句话。

00:33:14.440 --> 00:33:27.210
Just like, don't go for the widest audience, go for like the audience that is gonna really be tuned into your frequency of what you're saying and then the audience will grow.
就像是，不要去迎合最广泛的受众，而是去寻找那些真正能与你所表达的频率同频共振的受众，然后受众自然会壮大。

00:33:27.210 --> 00:33:28.769
I said a similar thing earlier.
我刚才也说过类似的话。

00:33:28.769 --> 00:33:30.970
It's like, we wrote for the smart audience.
就像我们是为聪明的受众写作的。

00:33:30.970 --> 00:33:34.529
We didn't think people had to forget week to week what the fuck was happening.
我们不认为人们一周接一周地会忘记他妈的到底发生了什么。

00:33:34.529 --> 00:33:36.930
Like it's, and it paid off.
这就像是，而且这得到了回报。

00:33:36.930 --> 00:33:39.589
Like there's some, there's something about this.
就像有些，这其中有些门道。

